WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.550
A balance service reads the payments topic, about three thousand payments a second, and keeps the balance of two million accounts.

2
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:14.596
If its machine dies, how does the next one get those balances back?

3
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:18.012
The simplest version keeps them in a map in memory.

4
00:00:18.012 --> 00:00:23.548
After a restart the map is empty, and the only way back is to read every payment again.

5
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:31.399
Seven days of payments is one point eight billion records: five hours to read, and older payments are already gone.

6
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:40.725
Kafka Streams keeps the table in a store on local disk, a small RocksDB database inside the service, so a read takes microseconds.

7
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:46.316
And every write to the store is also sent to a topic in Kafka, the store's changelog.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.324
Offset zero: account one pays in a hundred.

9
00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:54.577
The store gets account one at a hundred, and the changelog gets the same record.

10
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:57.245
Account two pays in fifty: a new row.

11
00:00:57.245 --> 00:00:59.188
Then account one pays thirty.

12
00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:06.221
The row changes in place to seventy, but a log can't change a record, so the changelog appends a new one.

13
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.600
Account one gets five back and reaches seventy five.

14
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.539
Then the app commits offset four: the next payment it will read.

15
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:17.162
Now the instance dies, and its disk goes with it.

16
00:01:17.162 --> 00:01:18.409
The store is gone.

17
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.431
Kafka still has everything that matters: the payments, the changelog and the committed offset.

18
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:26.312
A new instance takes over.

19
00:01:26.312 --> 00:01:31.992
Before it reads any payment, it replays the changelog from the start into a fresh store.

20
00:01:31.992 --> 00:01:34.445
For each account, the last value wins.

21
00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:39.839
Then it carries on from offset four: account two pays twenty and drops to thirty.

22
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:44.114
Over time, compaction keeps only the latest value per account.

23
00:01:44.114 --> 00:01:48.985
So a restore reads about two million records, not one point eight billion.

24
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.570
The table lives on local disk, so reads are fast.

25
00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:56.765
Its changelog lives in Kafka, so the table outlives the disk.
